      <title>Canada’s top court upholds Quebec law barring public sector workers from wearing religious symbols</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/27/canadas-top-court-upholds-quebec-law-barring-public-sector-workers-from-wearing-religious-symbols</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Closely watched decision exposes tensions between minority rights and the power of government to override those rights&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Canada’s top court has largely upheld a &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/nov/07/quebec-law-banning-religious-symbols-derails-womens-careers"&gt;controversial secularism law&lt;/a&gt; in the province of Quebec barring public sector workers from wearing conspicuous religious symbols, in a closely watched decision that exposes the tensions between minority rights and the power of government to override those rights.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In a 6-1 ruling &lt;a href="https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2026/41231/"&gt;released on Friday morning&lt;/a&gt;, the supreme court found Quebec was permitted to shield the law, Bill 21, from most court challenges on the grounds that it violated charter rights.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/27/canadas-top-court-upholds-quebec-law-barring-public-sector-workers-from-wearing-religious-symbols"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
