      <title>Newly released documents suggest CIA did more post-9/11 waterboarding than previously acknowledged</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/nov/27/newly-released-documents-cia-waterboarding-9-11</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Declassified documents appear to support belief that waterboarding was more common and may have occurred beyond two acknowledged sites&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Newly released documents suggest that the CIA waterboarded more detainees during the post-9/11 “war on terror” than previously acknowledged, utilizing a site north of Kabul, Afghanistan, for the practice of simulated drowning.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The agency has acknowledged waterboarding three men: Abu Zubaydah, believed to be a high-ranking member of al-Qaida; Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, suspected architect of the 11 September 2001 attacks; and Abd al-Rahim al-Nashiri, accused of planning the 2000 bombing of a navy destroyer.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/nov/27/newly-released-documents-cia-waterboarding-9-11"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
